HotkeyString.mcc/MUIA_HotkeyString_IX

    NAME
        MUIA_HotkeyString_IX

    FUNCTION
        Generate a description from selected fields of an input expression.

    INPUTS
        Initialize, set; struct InputXpression *. NULL is ignored. No local
        getter. The public header marks this attribute V12.

    NOTES
        Only IECLASS_RAWKEY expressions are handled. The setter constructs a
        temporary event from ix_Code and ix_Qualifier and calls the capture
        helper synchronously. It does not retain the expression or use masks,
        synonyms, and other expression fields.
        The helper accepts normal raw-key input when Snoop or Active is set;
        qualifier-only codes have separate acceptance. An inactive object with
        Snoop FALSE need not update its contents. Constructor processing
        defaults Snoop TRUE, then processes the first Snoop tag before the
        first IX tag regardless of their list order.
        Supply a valid readable expression for the call. Only the first IX tag
        is processed; the helper result is ignored. Read String_Contents to
        inspect the resulting text. This attribute does not establish a
        round-trip InputXpression conversion.
HotkeyString.mcc/MUIA_HotkeyString_Snoop

    NAME
        MUIA_HotkeyString_Snoop

    FUNCTION
        Enable or disable capture by this subclass.

    INPUTS
        Initialize, set; BOOL. Default TRUE. No local getter: OM_GET delegates
        this attribute to BetterString.

    NOTES
        Nonzero enables capture; zero disables it. Only the first Snoop tag in
        a message is used. Capture from the registered handler also requires
        Active. FALSE leaves normal editing to the inherited BetterString
        event handler; it does not make content immutable.
        While active, changing this flag overwrites Window_DisableKeys with a
        nonzero mask or zero. Activation uses a different nonzero mask. No
        previous mask is saved. While inactive, this setter does not change
        window keys.
        Snoop remains enabled after capture unless the application changes it.
        For one-shot capture, an application can clear Snoop in response to
        String_Contents, but that notification also occurs for programmatic
        changes. Do not infer the event source from it. 
